      {
        "heading": "5 DISCUSSION",
        "text": "In this work, we draw inspiration from biological synaptic plasticity and propose to incorporate different forms of plasticity into RNNs. We highlight the advantage of neuromodulated plasticity by comparing plastic RNNs against non-plastic ones on a range of challenging memory and fewshot learning tasks. In resonance with hypotheses from neuroscience (Magee & Grienberger, 2020; Martin et al., 2000; Neves et al., 2008), we show that adopting plasticity in RNNs improves their memory performance and helps them to learn from observations quickly. Moreover, we go beyond the traditional Hebbian plasticity and design a novel gradient-based plasticity where the model can flexibly adapt its weights with a self-generated target. Our experiments illustrate the feasibility of training RNNs capable of doing gradient updates where both the learning rule and the internal loss function are meta-trained.\nFor neuroscientists, our work provides a computational framework that offers insights into the functions of different plasticity rules in the brain, which is still challenging to directly test in animal experiments (Neves et al., 2008; Magee & Grienberger, 2020). Our results suggest that local Hebbian plasticity could be sufficient to provide robust memory storage and enable the brain to do simple forms of learning. These observations align with the fact that Hebbian plasticity is a prominent feature in hippocampal synapses and that the hippocampus is vital for forming episodic memory (Neves et al., 2008; Martin et al., 2000). However, results on the few-shot regression task show an example of the intrinsic limitation of local learning rules. Global plasticity rules are probably required for agents to learn and adapt to the complex dynamics in real-world environments.\nDespite the promising results, training plastic RNNs with the current deep learning paradigm comes with challenges. Plastic weights are intrinsically unstable, and methods that stabilize the model (e.g., clip weights, normalization) could also limit the model\u2019s capability. In addition, training plastic RNNs with back-propagation requires the plastic weight at each step to be stored in the computational graph, causing extensive memory usage on GPUs. As a result, applying plastic RNNs in more challenging settings (e.g., large-scale language modeling or complex RL environments) takes more engineering effort. Nevertheless, it is important to note that we have only explored a small part of the vast design space of plasticity rules. We hope our work can encourage future researchers to further improve the engineering framework and explore the benefit of plasticity in an even more comprehensive range of tasks."
